import { typographyTolerance, rasterRecognitionPolicy } from './bitmap-evidence.mjs';

export function editableScenePrompt(W,H,paper){
  return typographyTolerance+rasterRecognitionPolicy+'Reconstruct this image as an editable vector scene. The reference image is the source of truth. Return STRICT JSON only, with schema {"canvas":{"width":'+W+',"height":'+H+'},"regions":[{"id":"panel_1","kind":"chart|table|flow|panel|photo","x":0,"y":0,"w":100,"h":100}],"nodes":[...]}. COMPLETENESS CONTRACT: This is an exact tracing task, not a schematic summary. Output every visible instance, including repeated small details. Apply category-specific rules only where that content appears: connected diagrams require every visible node, edge, arrowhead and label with their actual topology and opacity; icons and illustrations require their visible outlines and interior components; charts require axes, ticks, legends and data marks; tables require a shared editable grid, cell fills and each cell text; repeated groups require their individual contents. Do not assume any category exists or impose a standard icon anatomy. Preserve actual panel fills, heading backgrounds and badges where present. Measure each outer panel and inner card corner: for every visibly rounded rectangle include a nonzero radius matching the source; never omit radius and silently turn a rounded frame square. Preserve frame stroke width, dash pattern, and the exact overlap of header bands with rounded frames in back-to-front paint order. Transcribe mathematical glyphs visually: use separate text nodes for visibly raised/lowered scripts, fraction numerator and denominator, and a line for each fraction bar; do not expose LaTeX commands or literal markup absent from the image. Match visible font family, weight, glyph height and line wrapping, including serif headings and multiline labels. If the exact font family cannot be established from the pixels, use the matching generic serif or sans-serif family instead of guessing a named decorative font. Estimate text size from visible ink and preserve readable layout; slight font or size differences are acceptable under the typography acceptance policy. Before emitting each region, count its visible nodes, edges, cards, icon parts, table rows and columns against the source and include every one; do not treat a descriptive text node as geometry. There is no target node count and no need to economize by discarding detail. First inspect the entire source at its exact pixel dimensions and locate every panel boundary. Systematically inspect the image from top to bottom, left to right, then revisit dense charts, tables, legends, icons and small labels. Measure all coordinates against the FULL supplied canvas, never against a mentally resized preview. Verify each panel boundary and every object center against the original image before emitting JSON. Preserve the measured panel proportions, whitespace and alignment; never redesign the layout. Use the independently collected source checklist to provide the COMPLETE object catalog and semantic regions. Choose the region count from actual content, without a quota. Keep each entire chart (axes, ticks, legend, data and title), table, or connected local flow in ONE region. Prefer flat non-overlapping regions covering all content, including captions. Assign node owner to its region id; global connectors may remain unowned. Independently assign sourceItemId to every node that belongs to an item in the supplied source inventory, using that exact inventory item id. Choose the most specific item that owns the primitive; an arrow shaft and its heads share their connector item id. This sourceItemId is replacement lineage, not a scene region id or an invented label. Use null only for global backgrounds or objects genuinely absent from the inventory. Retain this lineage even when guessed geometry crosses the inventory item bounds. This is the WHOLE IMAGE object catalog. Give every object a unique string id; retain each cross-region arrow, icon and text label exactly once as a complete object. Every node MUST have a string "type" field, for example {"type":"rect","x":1,"y":2,"w":3,"h":4}; never nest it as {"rect":{...}}. Use pixel coordinates in the supplied canvas. Extract every visible primitive separately: panel/group rectangles, rounded boxes, dashed frames, circles, graph nodes, individual small squares, arrows/lines, connectors, paths, and every readable text label. Transcribe each visible text line exactly, preserving capitalization, punctuation, mathematical symbols and line breaks. Use ONE text node for each complete continuous label or line, except visibly positioned mathematical scripts, numerators and denominators which require separate measured text nodes as specified above; never split a word or label into several text nodes at a crop edge, duplicate its overlapping characters, or combine unrelated nearby labels. Estimate fontSize from visible glyph height, not the surrounding box. Give the line a tight x,y,w,h bounding box that does not overlap other labels. If text content cannot be read, do not invent characters. Preserve all independently visible surrounding boxes, marks, connectors and geometry; uncertainty about text never permits omitting the object or its region. Text must be OCR transcription, not a description. Inspect dense modules for short connectors, dashed returns, repeated tiny boxes and axis ticks before returning JSON. Keep adjacent boxes, graph nodes and arrows as separate objects. For curved arrows and rings use path d with SVG M/L/C/Q/A/Z commands; preserve true arcs and curves rather than polygonal approximations. For rotated text set rotation in degrees. For 90/270-degree labels, x,y,w,h MUST describe the visible box AFTER rotation (a vertical label has a narrow w and tall h), not a horizontal layout box; retain the visible box center. For text following a ring or arc use a text node with textPath:{d:"M ... A ...",startOffset:0.5}; keep the text transcribed as editable characters. Treat each small icon, chart, and dense module as a collection of distinct primitives. Assign each outline, interior mark, text label, and connector its own unique ID; preserve the full geometry of every object across crop boundaries. This global catalog establishes stable object IDs; later patches may add genuinely missed objects. Do not reduce this first pass to a layout skeleton. Inspect every region and include small internal lines and shapes now; do not defer missing objects to later crop requests. For 3D-looking bars, cubes, prisms, or beveled objects, draw the front, top, and side faces as separate closed polygons with their own fill colors and edges; never flatten them to a rectangle. Preserve geometry and layer order. Do not return a single full-canvas bitmap as a substitute. For every line or arrow, both endpoints must coincide with visible endpoints in the supplied source. Never use (0,0), a crop corner, or a crop edge as a placeholder endpoint. Do not invent unseen line geometry; retain all confirmed visible segments and adjacent shapes even when a connection is uncertain. Node types are rect {x,y,w,h,radius,fill,stroke,strokeWidth,dash}, ellipse {x,y,w,h,fill,stroke,strokeWidth}, line {x,y,x2,y2,stroke,strokeWidth,arrow,dash}, polyline {points,closed,fill,stroke,strokeWidth}, path {d,fill,stroke,strokeWidth}, text {x,y,w,h,text,fontSize,fill,fontFamily,bold,italic,anchor,rotation,textPath}, and image {x,y,w,h,href}. Apply the photographic content exception above; image nodes use measured source crop bounds and an empty href, which the application fills from the original pixels. For EACH region check in order: outer frames and backgrounds; text; icon outlines and internal lines; connectors and arrowheads; axes and every tick; legends; every repeated small square; every face of 3D objects. Enumerate repeated instances individually, never substitute a representative or summary. Before submitting, revisit every region and category against the original and add omissions. Keep z-order from background to foreground. Use compact JSON with no commentary or repeated descriptions. Omit optional properties only when their default preserves appearance; retain geometry, IDs, owners, text, necessary styles and paint order. Colors may be hex. Keep coordinates and dimensions inside the canvas. Paper context, used only to disambiguate text, follows:\n'+String(paper||'').slice(0,1800);
}
